Some of the classical considerations in a CubeSat project are the following:

- the number of U should be as small as possible (smaller will reduce cost);

- the number of ground stations should be as small as possible;

- the duration of the mission should be maximal.

A possible semi-automated solution for performing trade-offs or design iterations throughout the design process is to consider the dependency graph/matrix as a task-planning problem. As an illustration, and for the sake of clarity, let us consider Figure 1 as a reference. Different "high level" tasks (not necessarily in this order) should be achieved in order to achieve the preliminary design of a CubeSat, such as for example:

- an orbit must be selected;

- a number of ground stations must be defined;

- the type of antenna must be set;

- the number of panels and batteries must be defined.

The next sections describe why we are talking about hierarchical planning when discussing the aforementioned preliminary Cube-Sat design as a semi-automated process that benefits from known unknowns (knowledge that in general arises thanks to expertise in the domain).

- setting the intermediary parameters with their initial default value (i.e.: antenna type is undefined, or by default, VHF as we know that it will be the less constrained type of communication);

- setting the constraints values (e.g: size of the payload, required data rate...);

- setting the root task ("design the mission").

- Therefore, from the design point of view:

- Design parameters are to be set through abstract tasks: e.g.: (d_nbGroundStation grd_1)

- Intermediary parameters are initially set in the HTN problem, and solved by the planning process: e.g.: (i_sc_power p_undefined)

CE complements both paradigms by accelerating design cycles through collaborative, multidisciplinary sessions. Unlike the sequential V-model, CE ensures that all domains are engaged in parallel, fostering rapid trade-offs and early detection of inconsistencies. CE has become the standard practice during early phases (0/A), with successful implementations such as NASA's Team-X [4] and ESA's Concurrent Design Facility [5, 9].

Despite its success, CE alone remains largely confined to preliminary phases, as it lacks the digital continuity to extend its benefits across the full lifecycle. Here, the integration with DDSE and MBSE becomes critical:

- CE provides the collaborative and iterative backbone;

- MBSE ensures structured modelling and rigorous traceability;

- DDSE guarantees consistency of engineering data, budgets, and requirements in real time.

This combined approach establishes a comprehensive and agile framework, capable of managing complexity, improving transparency, and extending the benefits of concurrent design to later lifecycle phases. The following section presents the proposed Concurrent Data-Driven Model-Based framework, developed to address these challenges.

The flight profile of BOREALIS must fulfil the strict requirements set by (i) mission goals, (ii) performance of commercial off-the-shelf (COTS) components, (iii) launch providers of rideshare opportunities, and in particular for the Vega-C [37,38], and the 5-year rule set by the FCC [39]. These requirements can be translated into the following mission profile specifications:

1. To distinguish the effects associated with microgravity and cosmic radiation, the satellite will have to operate in orbits in which the intensity of the cosmic radiation is significantly different;

2. The deployment orbit must be compatible with the rideshare performance of Vega-C (and eventually other launch vehicles);

3. Within 5 years from the end of the mission, the satellite will no longer have to orbit in LEO (below 2000 km); the TID collected during the operative mission will be limited to ensure the survival of the microbial cultures and the operativity of on-board devices and subsystems.

